A real flow is never a single request. You authenticate, create something, act on
it, verify the result holds together, and tidy up afterwards. This scenario does all
of that — and shows how a **dynamic auth profile**, **value extraction**, a
**CEL invariant**, and **setup/teardown phases** compose into one readable file.

## The auth profile

Token handling lives in `curlew.yaml` as a named profile. The profile runs a login
collection, caches the token, and re-runs on a 401 — so individual collections never
deal with credentials.

```yaml
# curlew.yaml
auth_profiles:
  shopper:
    type: dynamic
    collection: "auth/shopper-login.yaml"
    extract: "access_token"
    cache_ttl: 600
    refresh_on_failure: true
```

```yaml
# auth/shopper-login.yaml
name: Shopper login
requests:
  - name: Login
    request:
      method: POST
      url: "{{base_url}}/auth/login"
      body:
        email: "{{shopper_email}}"
        password: "{{shopper_password}}"   # name contains "password" → auto-redacted
    assertions:
      status: 200
    extract:
      access_token: "$.access_token"
```

## The collection

`setup` runs first (and is the only place a failure aborts the main phase),
`requests` is the body of the test, and `teardown` always runs — even if the
main phase fails — so your fixtures don't leak.

```yaml
# collections/checkout.yaml
name: Checkout flow
description: Create a cart, place an order, verify totals, clean up.

setup:
  - name: Create cart
    auth: shopper
    request:
      method: POST
      url: "{{base_url}}/carts"
      headers:
        Authorization: "Bearer {{access_token}}"
    assertions:
      status: 201
    extract:
      cart_id: "$.id"

requests:
  - name: Add item to cart
    auth: shopper
    request:
      method: POST
      url: "{{base_url}}/carts/{{cart_id}}/items"
      headers:
        Authorization: "Bearer {{access_token}}"
      body:
        sku: "WIDGET-1"
        qty: 2
    assertions:
      status: 200

  - name: Place order
    required: true                 # nothing downstream makes sense if this fails
    auth: shopper
    request:
      method: POST
      url: "{{base_url}}/carts/{{cart_id}}/checkout"
      headers:
        Authorization: "Bearer {{access_token}}"
        Idempotency-Key: "{{$uuid}}"
    assertions:
      status: 201
      body:
        "$.status":
          equals: "confirmed"
        "$.line_items":
          length: 1
      cel:
        # Cross-field invariant the operator catalogue can't express:
        - "response.body.total == response.body.subtotal + response.body.tax"
        - "response.body.line_items.all(i, i.qty > 0)"
    extract:
      order_id: "$.id"

teardown:
  - name: Cancel test order
    auth: shopper
    request:
      method: POST
      url: "{{base_url}}/orders/{{order_id}}/cancel"
      headers:
        Authorization: "Bearer {{access_token}}"
    assertions:
      status: 204
```

## Run it

```bash
curlew run collections/checkout.yaml --env staging
```

## What you get

```
Collection: Checkout flow

Setup:
  ✓ Create cart  201  181ms

  ✓ Add item to cart  200  96ms
  ✓ Place order  201  143ms

Teardown:
  ✓ Cancel test order  204  74ms

────────────────────────────────
  4 request(s): 4 passed, 0 failed (494ms)
```

<Callout type="output">
	Output is modeled on Curlew's documented terminal format — this flow targets a
	private staging API, so it can't run against a public endpoint.
</Callout>

The token is fetched once and reused across every request via the `shopper` profile;
`cart_id` and `order_id` flow forward through extraction; the CEL block catches a
billing bug (`total` drifting from `subtotal + tax`) that no status code would reveal.

<Callout type="tip">
	Because <code>Place order</code> is <code>required: true</code>, a failure there
	skips the rest of the main phase — but teardown still runs, so the test order is
	always cleaned up.
</Callout>
